## Reporting an error

Email <a href="mailto:contact@ageayurveda.com">contact@ageayurveda.com</a> with the page URL
and what is wrong. Useful things to include, if you have them: the correct information, and
where it comes from.

We are particularly interested in:

- **Botanical misidentification.** Classical names map onto species inconsistently and
  substitute species are widely used. If a binomial on a page is wrong for the name it sits
  under, we want to know.
- **Misattributed classical references.** If a page credits a text with something it does not
  contain, that is the most damaging kind of error here and the one we most want reported.
- **Misreported research.** If a study summary overstates or misstates what the paper found.
- **Transliteration and vernacular names**, including regional names we have missed.

## What we do with it

Verified corrections are made to the page and noted in the log below with the date. Where a
correction changes something substantive, the page's last-reviewed date is updated.

Where we cannot verify a correction either way, we would rather remove the disputed statement
than keep an unverifiable one. Several fields on this site are blank for exactly that reason;
see the known limitations on <a href={rel('/how-we-source/')}>how we source</a>.

## Correction log

Oldest first, each naming the page and what changed.

### 8 October 2026: two editorial promises amended

Our <a href={rel('/editorial-standards/')}>editorial standards</a> promised "No page exists
because a product needed one", and <a href={rel('/about/')}>about</a> promised "There is no
other commercial content on the site." Both were true when written and both are now partly
untrue: this site has begun publishing pages that state what named manufacturers, ours and
other companies', publish about a classical formulation, set against what the
<cite>Ayurvedic Formulary of India</cite> specifies.

Rather than leave the promise standing and hope nobody checked, both passages were rewritten to
describe what is now true, and the five rules those comparison pages follow were added to the
standards: the conflict-of-interest disclosure comes first on the page, the comparison is
organised by formulation and never brand against brand, every figure is the manufacturer's own
published figure with the date it was read, there is no score or rank or winner, and the
correction offer runs against our own interest. What did not change: no advertising, no
sponsorship, no affiliate links, and no money from any manufacturer named anywhere on this
site.

Amending a promise quietly would be worse than having made it, which is why this entry exists.

### 8 October 2026: dose figures put on a source, 50 formulation pages

Every formulation page states a dose. Those figures carried no source: of the 51 formulations
where we hold the <cite>Ayurvedic Formulary of India</cite>'s own dose, three of our figures
matched it, and no page attributed its figure to anything in particular. The dose is the most
consequential claim on these pages and it was the least sourced thing on the site.

What changed, by case:

- **25 pages now state the formulary's figure** instead of ours, cited to part and entry number,
  with the schedule and vehicle unchanged. The largest change is
  <a href={rel('/formulation/arogyavardhini-vati/')}>Arogyavardhini Vati</a>, where our
  125-250&nbsp;mg became the formulary's 250-500&nbsp;mg; the others move by less. Each page says
  what it previously stated.
- **5 kashayam pages keep their figure.** The formulary's number for a decoction is the weight of
  powder used to prepare it, not the volume taken, so the two are not comparable and are no longer
  presented as though they were.
- **8 pages keep their figure** because the formulary states a different unit, a weight against a
  volume or a powder weight against a tablet count, and relating them needs a density or a tablet
  mass the formulary does not give.
- **5 pages had no figure** and now state the formulary's.
- **<a href={rel('/formulation/mahasudarshan-churna/')}>Mahasudarshan Churna</a> no longer claims
  to be listed in the formulary**, because it is not. The formulary carries Sudarsana Curna and no
  maha- entry, and the two are different formulations. Its composition table is now labelled as
  the formulary's nearest entry, shown for comparison, rather than as this drug's own.

Six attribution errors were corrected in the same pass: pages that credited a dose to a classical
text the formulary contradicts. <a href={rel('/formulation/hinguvachadi-churna/')}>Hinguvachadi
Churna</a> was credited to the Sahasrayogam where the formulary gives Astangahrdaya, and
<a href={rel('/formulation/guloochyadi-kashayam/')}>Guloochyadi Kashayam</a> likewise where it
gives Sarngadharasamhita. The underlying mistake was general: these lines attributed a *dose* to a
classical text, when a classical text is the source of the *formula* and the formulary is what
states doses.

None of this was reported to us. It was found by checking our own dose attributions against the
formulary, and it is logged here because a correction nobody reported still changed what the pages
say.

### 10 October 2026: what the DOI resolves to, restated

<a href={rel('/datasets/')}>Datasets</a> carried a paragraph warning, against our own interest,
that the concept DOI resolved to a single 7.5&nbsp;MB archive of this project's repository rather
than to the data files, that no dataset had a DOI of its own, and that depositing the files was a
known gap not yet closed. That was accurate when written. It stopped being accurate on 10 October
2026, when v2.0.0 of the deposit replaced the archive with the 34 files themselves, each separately
downloadable, and the paragraph now describes that instead.

Two things did not change and the paragraph still says so. No dataset here has a DOI minted for it
alone, because a file being separately downloadable is not the same as a file being separately
identified, so the DOI continues to identify the work that contains these datasets rather than any
one of them. And the direct downloads on the page remain the same bytes as the archived copies,
checkable against the <a href={rel('/MANIFEST.json')}>manifest</a>'s sha256 for each file.

Logged because the old paragraph was a disclosure of a weakness, and quietly deleting a disclosure
once the weakness is fixed leaves no way to tell that it was ever disclosed. It was also nearly
rewritten a day early, on the belief that the deposit had been published when the public record
still showed one version holding one zip. <code>scripts/zenodo-claim.mjs</code> now reads the
claim out of the page, compares it against the live deposit on every build, and fails the build
when the two disagree, so the next time this page goes stale the build says so rather than a
person remembering to look.
